  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Kanuri\"\nsubtitle: \"Nigeria\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Kanuri are an African ethnic group. |\n| Where | They live mainly in northeastern Nigeria and also in Niger, Chad, Cameroon, and Sudan. |\n| How many | About 3 million Kanuri speakers live in Nigeria, plus about 200,000 Manga speakers; Niger has about 400,000 Kanuri. |\n| Language | Kanuri, a Saharan dialect continuum in the Nilo-Saharan language family. |\n| Religion | Predominantly Sunni Muslim. |\n| Known for | Kanem and Bornu empires · Farming and Chad Basin fishing · Trade and salt processing · Proverbs and oral literature · Leatherwork and quilted clothing |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Kanuri live mainly in the lands of the former Kanem and Bornu Empires. They have traditionally farmed, fished in the Chad Basin, traded, and processed salt. Their culture includes leatherwork, quilted clothing, proverbs, and written and oral literature. They are predominantly Sunni Muslim and have used Ajami Arabic writing for at least four hundred years.\n\n## Material culture\n\nKanuri material culture includes quilted cotton clothing, detailed leatherwork, iron tools and weapons, pottery, calabash objects, and jewelry.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nNamed textile traditions are not documented, but museum records show quilted cotton and patterned leather objects.\n\n- **Quilted coat** — A white cotton coat has concentric-circle quilting, red edging, a wide collar, leather buttons, and plaited leather loops.\n- **Quilted cap** — A coloured cloth cap is quilted and finished with tassels.\n- **Leatherwork belt** — A belt has 12 pouches with geometric decoration and hanging leather straps.\n- **Leatherwork cushion** — A round leather cushion has geometric patchwork on top and fringe around the edge.\n- **Plaited-leather container** — A cylindrical tin container is covered with plaited leatherwork and has a cover.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe records show quilted clothing, leather belts, neck and ear ornaments, plaited hair, and facial scarification.\n\n- **White quilted coat** — The coat has red edging, a deep back split, a wide turned-down collar, leather buttons, and plaited leather loops.\n- **Quilted cap** — The coloured cloth cap is quilted and has tassels.\n- **Pouched leather belt** — The belt has 12 pouches and geometric decoration.\n- **Women’s shirt and neck ornament** — One photograph shows a woman wearing a shirt and a neck ornament with a disc at the front.\n- **Plaited hairstyle** — Photographs show hair plaited against the scalp or in a plait over the forehead.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nKanuri objects include iron weapons, riding gear, pottery, calabash vessels, a chess set, and leather bags.\n\n- **Iron dagger** — A dagger is made of iron.\n- **Arm dagger** — An arm dagger has an iron blade and a wooden sheath.\n- **Iron spur** — An iron spur has a cloth covering tied with leather.\n- **Red-slip pottery cup** — A pottery cup has a flared base and is covered in red slip.\n- **Leather chess board** — A leather chess board uses natural and red-dyed leather squares with wooden chess pieces.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nAdornment includes stone hair ornaments, neck and ear ornaments, styled hair, and facial scarification.\n\n- **Stone hair ornaments** — Women’s hair ornaments are made of cornelian and agate.\n- **Neck ornament** — Photographs show women wearing neck ornaments.\n- **Circular ear ornaments** — Women wear circular ear ornaments and other ear ornaments.\n- **Ear piercing** — One portrait shows holes in the ears for ear ornaments.\n- **Facial scarification** — Facial scarification is visible in one portrait.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources do not describe a festival calendar or life-cycle rites, but they connect Kanuri religious life with Sunni Islam and proverbs.\n\n- **Sunni Islam** — The Kanuri are predominantly Sunni Muslim and became Muslims in the 11th century.\n- **Proverb teaching** — Proverbs are used in Islam to explain social happenings and teach meanings.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe sources do not name Kanuri dishes or cooking methods, but they describe farming, fishing, trade, and salt processing.\n\n- **Chad Basin fishing** — Kanuri groups have traditionally fished in the Chad Basin.\n- **Salt processing** — Kanuri groups have traditionally processed salt.\n- **Bilma salt trade** — The Bilma subgroup is described as the dominant ethnic group in Bilma’s salt evaporation and trade industry.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nProverbs and stories carry social lessons, religious ideas, and memories of Kanuri history.\n\n- **Kanuri proverbs** — Wise old men create proverbs that teach beliefs, experiences, social attitudes, and lessons from daily life.\n- **Trickster ground-squirrel** (*kə̀nyérì*) — Koelle recorded the trickster ground-squirrel, but mistakenly identified it as a weasel.\n- **Kanuri oral literature collection** — Koelle’s 1854 book contains Kanuri proverbs, stories, fables, and historical fragments with English translations.\n- **Sef origin tradition** — Kanuri tradition says that Sef, son of Dhu Ifazan of Yemen, arrived in Kanem in the ninth century and founded the Sayfawa dynasty.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nKanuri is a tonal Nilo-Saharan language with several dialects, Arabic and Latin writing systems, and a mainly Sunni Muslim population.\n\n- **Manga Kanuri** — Manga Kanuri is one of the main Kanuri dialects.\n- **Yerwa Kanuri** — Yerwa Kanuri is one of the main Kanuri dialects.\n- **Ajami Arabic script** (*Ajami*) — Kanuri has been written in the Ajami Arabic script for at least four hundred years, especially in religious and court settings.\n- **Modified Latin script** — Kanuri is also written in a modified Latin script.\n- **Standardized romanized orthography** — A standardized romanized orthography was approved in Maiduguri in 1975.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *kə̀nyérì* — trickster ground-squirrel\n- *Ajami* — Arabic script used for Kanuri\n- *Kanuri* — Kanuri language and people\n- *Manga Kanuri* — A main Kanuri dialect\n- *Yerwa Kanuri* — A main Kanuri dialect\n- *Sayfawa* — Dynasty named in Kanuri tradition\n- *Kanem* — Historic empire and region\n- *Bornu* — Historic empire and region\n- *Nilo-Saharan* — Language family of Kanuri\n- *Saharan* — Dialect-continuum classification\n- *Ajami Arabic script* — Arabic writing system used for Kanuri\n- *Manga* — Kanuri dialect and subgroup\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Peter Fuchs, *Fachi: Sahara-Stadt der Kanuri*, 2 vols., Franz Steiner Verlag Wiesbaden, 1989.\n- Peter Fuchs, *Fachi: Das Brot der Wüste*, Franz Steiner Verlag Wiesbaden, 1983.\n- Dierk Lange, “Ethnogenesis from within the Chadic state: Some Thoughts on the History of Kanem-Borno,” *Paideuma: Mitteilungen zur Kulturkunde* 39, 1993, 261–277.\n- Sigismund Koelle, *African Native Literature, or Proverbs, Tales, Fables, and Historical Fragments in the Kanuri or Bornu Language*, 1854.\n- [Kanuri people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanuri_people)\n- [Kanuri culture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanuri_culture)\n- [Kanuri language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanuri_language)\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription for this country is included in the supplied sources.\n",
